\section{Inference-Time Scaling with $K$}
\label{sec:k_scaling}

Figure~\ref{fig:kvalue} plots task success rate against the number of VLA samples $K$.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig3_kvalue.pdf}
\caption{Success rate (\%) vs.\ number of VLA samples $K$ on LIBERO-Object (obs\_only, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$). Reference lines: VLA-only ($11.0$\%, 8 seeds) and Argmin-only ($12.3$\%, 6 seeds). Error bars: $\pm$1 std. Note: figure reference lines reflect seed sets used at generation time; Table~\ref{tab:main} reports final aggregated statistics.}
\label{fig:kvalue}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:k_ablation} reports the full $K$-ablation on both benchmarks.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Success rate (\%) vs.\ number of VLA samples $K$ on LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Spatial. Plan~A uses $K{=}2$ with selective CRM; all other CRM rows use unconditional correction at fixed $K$. Plan~A matches CRM $K{=}20$ with 90\% fewer VLA forward passes.}
\label{tab:k_ablation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & Spatial (\%) \\
\midrule
VLA-only & 1 & $11.0 \pm 1.3$ & $29.4 \pm 1.5$ \\
\textbf{Plan~A (ours)} & \textbf{2} & $\mathbf{18.3 \pm 2.1}$ & $\mathbf{35.0 \pm 1.0}$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 5 & $16.0 \pm 2.2$ & $34.5 \pm 2.1$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 10 & $16.8 \pm 1.8$ & $34.4 \pm 1.5$ \\
CRM obs\_only & 20 & $18.8 \pm 1.0$ & $34.3 \pm 0.6$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Performance improves with $K$ but saturates: Object rises from $11.0$\% ($K{=}1$) to $16.0$\% ($K{=}5$, $+$5.0\,pp) to $16.8$\% ($K{=}10$, $+$0.8\,pp) to $18.8$\% ($K{=}20$, $+$2.0\,pp, 4 seeds). On Spatial, CRM at $K{=}5{/}10{/}20$ clusters within 0.2\,pp ($34.3$--$34.5$\%), indicating complete saturation.
Plan~A ($K{=}2$) matches CRM $K{=}20$ on Object ($18.3$\% vs.\ $18.8$\%) and exceeds it on Spatial ($35.0$\% vs.\ $34.3$\%), using 90\% fewer VLA forward passes. This confirms that the gains derive from learned correction, not brute-force sampling.
At $K{=}1$, CRM correction alone falls below the VLA-only baseline (10.3\% vs.\ $11.0$\%), because the single sample lacks argmin centering and shifts outside the CRM's training distribution. Multi-sample selection is an enabling condition for effective correction.
The diminishing-returns pattern is loosely analogous to inference-time compute scaling in LLMs~\cite{snell2024scaling}, though the mechanism differs: in our setting, additional samples improve the starting point for correction rather than enabling better selection.
With batched inference (shared KV cache), $K{=}10$ sampling takes ${\sim}$255\,ms, plus $<$\,1\,ms for CRM inference and argmin selection. See Appendix~\ref{sec:latency} for the full timing breakdown.

